## A machine that plays the guessing game

<At levels="simple">

In 1951, Claude Shannon played this game with people, one letter at a time, to measure how
predictable English is. Very, it turned out.<Cite id="shannon1951" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

In 1951, Claude Shannon ran an experiment that looks a lot like this one. He showed people the
start of a passage and asked them to guess the next letter, again and again, and used how well they
did to measure how predictable written English is. The answer: very.<Cite id="shannon1951" />

</At>

<At levels="simple">

The model above, GPT-2 small, plays the same game. It doesn't commit to one answer: it gives
every possible next word a chance, and spreads its bets across *floor*, *bed*, *couch*, and many
more.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A large language model plays the same game. The model above is GPT-2 small, which OpenAI released
in 2019.<Cite id="radford2019" /><Cite id="gpt2-model-card" /> It read the words *The cat sat on
the* and gave every possible continuation a chance. Notice that it doesn't commit to one answer.
It spreads its bets: *floor*, *bed*, and *couch* are all likely, and no single option gets even
one chance in ten.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Chances, written down

The game has an exact form. Write a text as tokens $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n$: the chunks GPT-2
really reads, which <ChapterRef to="tokens" /> explains. For each place $i$, the model gives the
chance of token $x_i$ given every token before it, written $P(x_i \mid x_1, \dots, x_{i-1})$. The
chance of a whole text is those chances multiplied together:

$$
P(x_1, \dots, x_n) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} P(x_i \mid x_1, \dots, x_{i-1})
$$

where $\prod$ means multiplying, for every place $i$ from 1 to $n$.<Cite id="radford2019" /> The product
shrinks fast. After *The cat sat on the*, *floor* gets 7.6%, and a comma after *floor* gets 26%, so
the model's favorite two-token continuation has a chance of only about 2%.

Spreading its bets is normal. Here the 50 likeliest next tokens add up to only about 64%; the other
50,207 share the rest. Shannon measured that spread as **entropy**, in bits:

$$
H = -\sum_{i} p_i \log_2 p_i
$$

where $p_i$ is the chance of outcome $i$. One bit is one fair yes-or-no question; a choice between
eight equally likely outcomes is 3 bits.<Cite id="shannon1948" /> From his guessing experiments,
Shannon estimated that printed English carries about one bit per letter, given the 100 letters
before it.<Cite id="shannon1951" />

</At>

## Over and over

One guess is only one word. To write a sentence, the model adds its pick to the end of the text and
plays the game again with the longer text. Step through the first few rounds:

<RecordedGeneration name="the-cat-sat" title="The guessing game, repeated" maxSteps={4} />

That's the whole trick, repeated: guess, pick, add, repeat. Everything else in this site explains
how the guessing works, and how much can be built on top of it.

<RecapCard>

- A large language model is a machine that predicts the next piece of text.
- It doesn't pick one answer. It gives every possible continuation a probability.
- To write more, it adds a pick to the text and predicts again, over and over.

<At levels="deep">

- The chance of a whole text is each token's chance, given the ones before it, multiplied
  together.

</At>
</RecapCard>

